    <section class="py-16 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mb-8">Frequently asked questions on laboratory planning</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group" open>
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">How much contingency should I allow for permitting a lab container?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Allow at least <strong>six to eight weeks</strong> of contingency for each permitting step. For a BSL-2 laboratory, the opinion of the Central Committee on Biological Safety (ZKBS) alone takes eight to sixteen weeks, and the building permit six to twelve weeks. These periods do not always run in parallel.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">What is the difference between a BSL-2 and a BSL-3 lab container?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><strong>BSL-2</strong> applies to Risk Group 2 organisms and requires, among other things, a negative pressure of at least 20 pascals, a Class II microbiological safety cabinet and autoclavable waste routes. <strong>BSL-3</strong> for Risk Group 3 requires multi-stage airlocks, interlocked double doors, HEPA filtration and autoclavable pass-throughs.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Can a lab container later be upgraded to a higher containment level?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">In a modularly planned lab container, the upgrade path is designed in from the outset: ventilation ducts are sized for later filtration, wall surfaces are coated for autoclave-compatible cleaning, and airlock doors are specified to the required openings. Conversion to BSL-2 standard is then possible within around <strong>four weeks</strong> – at roughly one third of the effort of a conventional solution.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">What infrastructure must be in place at the installation site?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Before delivery, a <strong>load-bearing substrate</strong>, electricity, water and wastewater connections, and access routes wide enough for a low-loader and crane must be ready. If the crane pad is missing or the access is too tight, the container cannot be set down – that costs weeks and unnecessary extra expense.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Why do mobile laboratories have an advantage in permitting?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">As temporary installations, mobile laboratories often fall under simplified permit procedures. In container construction, fabrication can also be started in parallel with the permitting phase: while the authority is reviewing, the basic structure is already taking shape in the factory. That saves real time on the overall project.</div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
